{"conjecture": "The set of execution paths eliminated by each change is never recorded, so the codebase retains a growing shadow of unreachable branches that future developers must still analyze as potentially live. Because static analysis cannot reliably distinguish a genuinely dead path from one activated only by rare runtime configurations, the cost of proving a proposed change is safe grows proportionally to the total number of branches rather than the reachable subset. The system thus becomes harder to change not because its real behavior is expanding, but because its *hypothesized* behavior—the union of all paths that cannot be cheaply disproven—expands with every edit that leaves residue behind."}
